section.para:
The detector works on any character sequence. In the text below, cafÃ© is what café becomes when its UTF-8 bytes are read through the Windows-1252 decoding, and U+FFFD is a replacement character left by a failed decode:
ArtifactDetector detector = new CursorArtifactDetector(); String text = "cafÃ© costs \uFFFD8";
for (TextArtifact artifact : detector.detect(text)) { System.out.println(artifact.type() + " " + artifact.span()); }
This prints one line per finding, with exact offsets into the original text:
mojibake [3..5) replacement [12..13)
The reverse mapping covers both Windows-1252 and ISO-8859-1. The latter preserves bytes 0x80 through 0x9F as C1 controls, so damaged text such as the UTF-8 euro sign read through Latin-1 is reported as one mojibake span, not a separate control. Controls outside the mojibake span remain separate findings. Text such as déjà vu produces no finding because its reverse mapping is not valid UTF-8. This check does not prove which decoder was used. ArtifactsManualExampleTest verifies the behavior shown here.

The default constructor reports every built-in type. A caller can limit findings to selected types with the set constructor:
ArtifactDetector textDamage = new CursorArtifactDetector(Set.of( TextArtifact.TYPE_MOJIBAKE, TextArtifact.TYPE_UNICODE_TAG));
List<TextArtifact> damage = textDamage.detect(text);
The set must be non-empty, contain no null elements, and contain only the constants in TextArtifact. It is copied during construction, so later changes to the supplied set do not alter the detector. ArtifactsManualExampleTest verifies this configuration.

			ArtifactAnnotator adapts the detector to the document pipeline. It
			requires no other layer and provides opennlp:artifacts, one
			annotation per finding with a TextArtifact value:

Document document = new ArtifactAnnotator()
    .annotate(Document.of("cafÃ© costs \uFFFD8"));

List<Annotation<TextArtifact>> artifacts = document.get(ArtifactAnnotator.ARTIFACTS);
// Two annotations; the first covers "Ã©" with type "mojibake".

			The offsets can be used to mask reported characters without changing the text
			length:

StringBuilder masked = new StringBuilder("cafÃ© costs \uFFFD8");
for (Annotation<TextArtifact> artifact : artifacts) {
    for (int i = artifact.span().getStart(); i < artifact.span().getEnd(); i++) {
        masked.setCharAt(i, '#');
    }
}
// masked.toString(): "caf## costs #8"

			ArtifactsManualExampleTest verifies the behavior shown here.

			Adjacent findings keep separate spans. Here, mojibake occupies offsets 6 to 8
			and the following NUL occupies offsets 8 to 9:

String text = "value " + new String(new int[] {0x00C2, 0x0080, 0x0000}, 0, 3);
Document document = new ArtifactAnnotator().annotate(Document.of(text));
List<Annotation<TextArtifact>> artifacts = document.get(ArtifactAnnotator.ARTIFACTS);
// artifacts.get(0): mojibake [6..8)
// artifacts.get(1): control [8..9)

			The original text remains unchanged. ArtifactsManualExampleTest
			verifies these spans through the document layer.
